83bc1b08e3c95a70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-32617" :question/id #uuid "b49c26b1-11bf-5006-a31c-56e1faf163c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-32617" :question/problem "Given that \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-32617/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-32617"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-32608/selection/421ba3cf61c86c7d854403490925f53920cd3c015c12342698571d9a74aca2fd" :answer/id #uuid "2b67308b-4195-57a4-958e-454fe560bf87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-32608/selection/ecf46090d