5b0b111e062a8dcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106773" :question/id #uuid "e3a53935-9a29-51e4-b6cc-60f8abed976b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106773" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106773/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106773"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106769/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1a9b6684-676a-5316-8b89-c21048f9817e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106769/selection/62a6fd1cf465955